markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Instructional Commitments

<img src="https://images.code.org/1cd0ec71458414495502b9598efd2028-image-1639773441510.37.01 PM.png" alt="Code.org AI bot character" style="border-radius:40px; float: left; padding-right: 2px; height: 150px; clear: both; margin: 0 20px 0px 10px;">

<br>

#### As part of this unit’s *instructional commitments*, students develop the habits and mindsets that connect *computational thinking, human-centered design, and responsible AI use*.

<div style="clear:both;"></div>

 <div
  style="
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 12px;
    margin: 16px 0;
    background: #b2dee3;
  "
>
  <img 
    src="https://images.code.org/59c9b8a356ff4e56ec8c802ba5718b37-image-1684269154206.png" 
    alt="A clipboard labeled TO DO" 
    width="40px"
    style="margin-right: 12px;"
  >

  <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 16px;">
    <strong>As you explore each commitment below, consider how these ideas will shape the way students design, build, and collaborate with AI in their web apps.</strong>
  </p>
</div>



<div style="clear:both;"></div>

## Computational Thinking

#### This unit deepens students’ computational thinking by shifting from isolated logic to structured, data-driven systems.

<!-- Grid container -->
<div
  style="
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 16px;
    margin: 10px 0;
  "
>
  <!-- Card 1 -->
  <div
    style="
      flex: 1 1 calc(33.333% - 16px);
      box-sizing: border-box;
      border: 2px solid #0093A4;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 12px;
      background: #ffffff;
      box-shadow: 2px 4px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);
      min-width: 220px;
    "
  >
    <details>
      <summary style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; cursor: pointer;">
        🧩 Decomposition
      </summary>
      <p style="margin: 8px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">
        <i>Students break down dataset-driven features into smaller steps, such as writing one function to retrieve an item, another to render it, and another to filter results. This reinforces the idea that complex systems are built from well-organized, reusable parts.</i>
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>

  <!-- Card 2 -->
  <div
    style="
      flex: 1 1 calc(33.333% - 16px);
      box-sizing: border-box;
      border: 2px solid #0093A4;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 12px;
      background: #ffffff;
      box-shadow: 2px 4px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);
      min-width: 220px;
    "
  >
    <details>
      <summary style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; cursor: pointer;">
      🧠 Algorithmic Reasoning
      </summary>
      <p style="margin: 8px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">
        <i>Students design algorithms that operate on collections, learning to search, sort, and filter data. By tracing inputs and outputs across multiple steps, they strengthen their ability to reason about how logic structures shape system behavior.</i>
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>

  <!-- Card 3 -->
  <div
    style="
      flex: 1 1 calc(33.333% - 16px);
      box-sizing: border-box;
      border: 2px solid #0093A4;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 12px;
      background: #ffffff;
      box-shadow: 2px 4px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);
      min-width: 220px;
    "
  >
    <details>
      <summary style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; cursor: pointer;">
       🎨 Abstraction
      </summary>
      <p style="margin: 8px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">
        <i> Students practice abstraction by deciding what properties to include when modeling real-world information as arrays and objects. They formalize these choices in a schema that defines fields, types, and constraints, which then serves as the foundation for both UI and program logic.</i>
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
</div>




<hr>

## Human-Centered Design and Systems Thinking

#### In this unit, students learn that data choices have broad impacts on users and systems.

<!-- Grid container -->
<div
  style="
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 16px;
    margin: 10px 0;
  "
>
  <!-- Card 1 -->
  <div
    style="
      flex: 1 1 calc(33.333% - 16px);
      box-sizing: border-box;
      border: 2px solid #0093A4;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 12px;
      background: #ffffff;
      box-shadow: 2px 4px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);
      min-width: 220px;
    "
  >
    <details>
      <summary style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; cursor: pointer;">
         🌐 Data as Part of the Experience
      </summary>
      <p style="margin: 8px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">
        <i> Students recognize that user experience depends not only on interface design but also on the integrity and representation of the underlying data. A polished interface built on biased or incomplete data can still create a poor experience.
</i>
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>

  <!-- Card 2 -->
  <div
    style="
      flex: 1 1 calc(33.333% - 16px);
      box-sizing: border-box;
      border: 2px solid #0093A4;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 12px;
      background: #ffffff;
      box-shadow: 2px 4px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);
      min-width: 220px;
    "
  >
    <details>
      <summary style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; cursor: pointer;">
        🖥️ Schema-Driven Design
      </summary>
      <p style="margin: 8px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">
        <i>By defining schemas, students see how data structures directly shape user-facing choices. For example, a field with predefined categories might map to a dropdown menu, while a numeric field could become a slider. The schema acts as a foundational plan that ensures alignment between the data, the program’s logic, and the user interface.
</i>
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>

  <!-- Card 3 -->
  <div
    style="
      flex: 1 1 calc(33.333% - 16px);
      box-sizing: border-box;
      border: 2px solid #0093A4;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 12px;
      background: #ffffff;
      box-shadow: 2px 4px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);
      min-width: 220px;
    "
  >
    <details>
      <summary style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; cursor: pointer;">
       🧠 Critical Reflection
      </summary>
      <p style="margin: 8px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">
        <i> To make reflection a tangible practice, students are encouraged to create simple “ethical charters.” This involves defining a mission statement and a few guiding principles to ensure their web app is fair, safe, and inclusive. This charter then informs their decisions about what data to include or exclude, whose perspectives might be missing, and what ripple effects their choices may cause.
</i>
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
</div>

<hr>

## AI Ethics and Safety 
#### The introduction of **Outline** mode in Web Lab foregrounds the need for accountability and judgment in AI use, while the unit’s focus on data provides a new context for ethical AI collaboration. 

<!-- AI Ethics and Safety Cards -->
<div
  style="
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 16px;
    margin: 10px 0;
  "
>
  <!-- Card 1 -->
  <div
    style="
      flex: 1 1 calc(33.333% - 16px);
      box-sizing: border-box;
      border: 2px solid #0093A4;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 12px;
      background: #ffffff;
      box-shadow: 2px 4px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);
      min-width: 220px;
    "
  >
    <details>
      <summary style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; cursor: pointer;">
        🤖 AI Collaboration for Data-Driven Design
      </summary>
      <p style="margin: 8px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">
        <i> Students will apply their AI collaboration skills to tasks central to this unit’s learning goals. They are encouraged to use AI as a thought partner to brainstorm data schemas, act as an “ethics consultant” to surface potential risks within a dataset, and generate initial data that conforms to their defined structure.</i>
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>

  <!-- Card 2 -->
  <div
    style="
      flex: 1 1 calc(33.333% - 16px);
      box-sizing: border-box;
      border: 2px solid #0093A4;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 12px;
      background: #ffffff;
      box-shadow: 2px 4px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);
      min-width: 220px;
    "
  >
    <details>
      <summary style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; cursor: pointer;">
        🔍 Judgment in Creative Modes
      </summary>
      <p style="margin: 8px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">
        <i>A core skill in this unit is learning to select the most appropriate AI mode for a given task. Students practice this judgment by deciding when it is most effective to use Outline to complete a pre-defined program structure versus using Guide to build piece-by-piece or Suggest to debug existing code.</i>
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>

  <!-- Card 3 -->
  <div
    style="
      flex: 1 1 calc(33.333% - 16px);
      box-sizing: border-box;
      border: 2px solid #0093A4;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 12px;
      background: #ffffff;
      box-shadow: 2px 4px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);
      min-width: 220px;
    "
  >
    <details>
      <summary style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; cursor: pointer;">
       ✅Accountability for Code
      </summary>
      <p style="margin: 8px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">
        <i>TWith Outline mode, students are accountable not only for the logic they write to complete TODOs, but also for understanding, testing, and verifying the surrounding scaffold generated by the AI. This responsibility extends to all AI-generated assets, including any data and schemas used in the project.</i>
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
</div>

<hr>

## Career Journeys
#### Activities are framed around real-world professional roles, helping students see design as a meaningful and practical career skill.

<!-- Career Journeys Cards -->
<div
  style="
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 16px;
    margin: 10px 0;
  "
>
  <!-- Card 1 -->
  <div
    style="
   flex: 1 1 calc(33.333% - 16px);
      box-sizing: border-box;
      border: 2px solid #0093A4;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 12px;
      background: #ffffff;
      box-shadow: 2px 4px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);
      min-width: 220px;
    "
  >
    <details>
      <summary style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; cursor: pointer;">
        💻UX Researcher
      </summary>
      <p style="margin: 8px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">
        <i>Students analyze how datasets affect the inclusivity and fairness of user experiences.</i>
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>

  <!-- Card 2 -->
  <div
    style="
      flex: 1 1 calc(33.333% - 16px);
      box-sizing: border-box;
      border: 2px solid #0093A4;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 12px;
      background: #ffffff;
      box-shadow: 2px 4px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);
      min-width: 220px;
    "
  >
    <details>
      <summary style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; cursor: pointer;">
      🧑‍💻 Product Manager</summary>
      <p style="margin: 8px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">
        <i> Students make design trade-offs, such as which dataset features to prioritize or which user needs to serve.</i>
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>


  <!-- Card 3 -->
  <div
    style="
 flex: 1 1 calc(33.333% - 16px);
      box-sizing: border-box;
      border: 2px solid #0093A4;
      border-radius: 10px;
      padding: 12px;
      background: #ffffff;
      box-shadow: 2px 4px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);
      min-width: 220px;
    "
  >
    <details>
      <summary style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; cursor: pointer;">
      🌐 Data-Focused Roles</summary>
      <p style="margin: 8px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">
        <i>Students begin to experience the responsibilities of data analysts and engineers, such as structuring information and evaluating dataset quality. These roles emphasize that data choices are foundational to building trustworthy systems.</i>
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
</div>

